<h3>Introductory Article</h3>
<h4>• Value:</h4>
<p>
    The City Opera House (Saigon Opera House) is an outstanding architectural and artistic work that carries significant historical, cultural, and aesthetic value. <br/>
Constructed beginning in 1898 and completed in 1900 in the French classical style, the theatre features an ornate façade, curved domed roofs, and numerous European-style sculptural details. <br/>
It once served as a cultural venue for the French colonial elite and later hosted many major political and cultural events. <br/>
Today, the opera house is a prestigious performing arts center presenting concerts, operettas, opera, and folk performances, contributing to the preservation and promotion of both traditional and contemporary cultural values. <br/>
More than an artistic landmark, the opera house is also a prominent tourist attraction and an architectural icon in the heart of the city, drawing large numbers of domestic and international visitors. <br/>
With its long history and refined architectural beauty, the City Opera House rightly stands as one of Ho Chi Minh City’s most treasured cultural heritage sites.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/nht2.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: tienphong.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Origins:</h4>
<p>
    The Ho Chi Minh City Opera House, originally known as the Saigon Opera House, was built at the end of the 19th century and stands as a quintessential example of French colonial architecture in Indochina. After the French took control of Cochinchina in 1863, the colonial authorities invited a French theater troupe to perform in Saigon to entertain the expeditionary forces. Initially, performances were held in a wooden hall inside the Admiral’s Palace, later moving to a temporary theater located where the Caravelle Hotel stands today. <br/>
In 1898, the construction of the Opera House officially began, and it was inaugurated on January 1, 1900, featuring classical French architecture, with elegant decorations, ornate carvings, and a strong European artistic spirit. <br/>
During the period between the two world wars, the theater remained active but gradually lost its appeal due to the emergence of new entertainment forms. In 1943, the façade was simplified to achieve a more “modern” style, but in 1944, the building was heavily damaged by bombings. After 1954, the venue was used as housing for French residents migrating from the North, and later converted into the National Assembly (and later the Lower House) of the Republic of Vietnam. <br/>
After the reunification of the country on April 30, 1975, the Opera House was restored to its original function as a performing arts venue. Notably, in 1998, during the 300th anniversary of Saigon – Ho Chi Minh City, the building underwent a major restoration, returning it to its original appearance with sculpted goddesses, garlands, and reliefs reinstated on its façade. In 2007, another comprehensive restoration project was approved, including roof replacement, seat restoration, floor tiling, sculpture refurbishment, and new artistic lighting, completed in late 2009. <br/>
After over 120 years, the Ho Chi Minh City Opera House stands not only as an architectural and artistic masterpiece but also as a living witness to the historical, cultural, and urban transformations of the city — a symbol of harmony between the past and the present, and between European elegance and Vietnamese spirit.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/nht3.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: wikipedia.com</div>
</div>
<h4>• Structural Value:</h4>
<p>
    The architectural value of the Ho Chi Minh City Opera House is reflected in the harmonious blend between European architectural artistry and the distinctive charm of late 19th-century Saigon. Built in 1900 by French architects Félix Olivier, Ernest Guichard, and Eugène Ferret, the structure follows the “flamboyant” architectural style typical of the French Third Republic. The façade was notably influenced by the Petit Palais in Paris, which was built around the same period. With its symmetrical design, large columns, vaulted roof, bas-reliefs, garlands, and statues of artistic muses, the building showcases a Beaux-Arts style infused with Imperial and Art Deco influences, creating a magnificent and sophisticated appearance. <br/>
Inside, the theatre originally had a capacity of 1,800 seats, including the ground floor and two balconies, and was equipped with a state-of-the-art sound and lighting system for its time. All interior decorative motifs and façade reliefs were designed by renowned French artists and transported to Vietnam for installation, ensuring both aesthetic and technical consistency. <br/>
However, in the past, the Opera House was once criticized for being overly ornate, leading to the removal of several sculptural details in 1943 to give it a “younger” and more modern look. It wasn’t until 1998, during the 300th anniversary of Saigon – Ho Chi Minh City, that the municipal government restored and reconstructed artistic elements such as the two goddess statues, garlands, and façade lanterns, with a total budget of around 25 billion VND. <br/>
Today, the Ho Chi Minh City Opera House stands not only as a unique architectural masterpiece of old Saigon but also as an architectural counterpart to the Hanoi Opera House — both inspired by the Opéra Garnier in Paris, yet each bearing its own distinctive character. With its elegant grandeur and rich history, the Opera House remains a cultural and architectural landmark, contributing significantly to the urban identity and artistic life of Ho Chi Minh City.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/nht4.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: vietnamnet.vn</div>
